    <statement><p>Let <m>\sigma</m> be the surface <m>2 x + 6 y + 5 z=8</m> in the first octant, oriented upwards. Let C be the oriented boundary of <m>\sigma</m>. Compute the work done in moving a unit mass particle around the boundary of <m>\sigma</m> through the vector field <m>F = \left(4x-7y\right)\boldsymbol{i}+\left(7y-5z\right)\boldsymbol{j}+\left(5z-4x\right)\boldsymbol{k}</m> using line integrals, and using Stokes' Theorem.</p>
<p>LINE INTEGRALS</p>
<p>Parameterize the boundary of <m>\sigma</m> positively using the standard form,  tv+P with <m>0\leq t \leq 1</m>, starting with the segment in the xy plane.</p>
<p><m>C_1</m> (the edge in the xy plane) is parameterized by <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>C_2</m> (the edge following <m>C_1</m>) is parameterized by <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>C_3</m> (the last edge) is parameterized by <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\int_{C_1}F\cdot dr =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\int_{C_2}F\cdot dr =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0005"/></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\int_{C_3}F\cdot dr =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0006"/></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\int_{C}F\cdot dr =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0007"/></p>
<p>STOKES' THEOREM</p>
<p><m>\sigma</m> may be parameterized by <m>r(x,y)=x\boldsymbol{i}+y\boldsymbol{j}+f(x,y)\boldsymbol{k}=</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0008"/></p>
<p><m>\mbox{curl } F =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0009"/></p>
<p><m>\frac{\partial r}{\partial x}\times\frac{\partial r}{\partial y} =</m> <fillin characters="30" name="AnSwEr0010"/></p>
<tabular bottom="none" left="none" right="none" top="none">
<row halign="left" valign="middle">
<cell><m>\displaystyle\iint\limits_\sigma (\mbox{curl } F)\cdot {\bf n}\hspace{1pt} dS=</m></cell>
<cell><fillin characters="4" name="AnSwEr0012"/>
